{
  "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/access-to-justice",
  "type": "Concept",
  "prefLabel": {
    "en": "access to justice"
  },
  "definition": {
    "en": "The right and real possibility of every person to turn to the courts and to other dispute-resolution mechanisms, and to participate in them effectively and on an equal basis, whether as a party, witness, victim or in any other capacity."
  },
  "scopeNote": {
    "en": [
      "It is not exhausted by formal judicial protection: it requires removing the economic, physical, communication and attitudinal barriers that impede it. For persons with disabilities, the CRPD guarantees it in art. 13, which requires procedural accommodations that are age- and gender-appropriate, accessibility and training for those working in the administration of justice; unlike reasonable accommodation, procedural accommodations are not subject to the disproportionate-burden limit. It also has a gender dimension – justice free from stereotypes for women victims of violence – and a childhood dimension – child-friendly justice for children and adolescents."
    ]
  },
  "notation": [
    "10.1"
  ],
  "broader": {
